Collect a number that could be used to enforce a (soft) quota, 
updating automatically as objects are received and removed.

`git annex info --json --bytes --show 'annex sizes of repositories'` cannot
be used for a quota because bad information could be put into the
git-annex branch. (Eg send objects to the server, then pull the git-annex
branch from the server to get its updates, and overwrite them.)

`git annex info --json --bytes --show 'local annex size'` is too slow
to query since it walks the object directory tree.

Also both of the above omit the size of keys w/o a stated size.
A quota needs to include the actual file size of such keys.

And this should also handle partially transferred objects. It would be
possible to count each chunk as it's received, and update the value
stored on disk after each megabyte or gigabyte. (Make the update granularity
tunable with a git config.)

This is complicated by concurrency. For example 2 processes can both drop
the same key at the same time and both succeed, so if both update the
number it would be wrong. Database.RepoSize already deals with much the
same problems for live size changes.

[[!tag projects/ICE4]]
